**********Source Check**********

The claim 'Neil Gust's songs `some specific titles` are included in `a certain type of release`.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The "Credits" section at the end of the document explicitly states that "All songs" on the album *Mic City Sons* were "written by Neil Gust or Elliott Smith". Additionally, within the detailed discussion of the songs, Neil Gust or Tony Lash explicitly identify "Low Flying Jets", "Rest My Head Against the Wall", "Eagle Eye", "Cruel Reminder", and "Blue Highway" as Neil Gust's songs, all of which are included on the *Mic City Sons* album. </accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim 'Neil Gust `struggled to articulate` the feeling of discovering `someone` was about to become `individually prominent`.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> Neil Gust explicitly discusses the feeling of Elliott Smith's solo career taking off and his own struggle with it, stating, "I felt like I was trying to catch up to his process. And because not only did his process sound really good, it was successful, it connected with other people. And so suddenly, he got on a roll." He further explains the impact on him: "While his solo stuff started to blow up, I got this feeling from some friends and definitely from people that we knew in the music industry that they just didn't have, they no longer had any use for me. You know that what they wanted was Elliott... It was just a pervasive feeling of becoming invisible." Regarding the articulation of this feeling, he notes that his song "Low Flying Jets" was about "losing confidence, and just feeling isolated," and directly states, "I wasn't saying to Elliott, 'You can't hear me.' But I was saying, 'I'm getting drowned out by all this noise.'" This shows he struggled to articulate these feelings directly but expressed them through his music, a form of struggle to articulate verbally. </accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim 'Neil Gust `assimilated` `a particular musical element` from Elliott Smith.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> Neil Gust states, "I learned so much about songwriting from him... I had to try to absorb his sort of melodic sensibility, which is not easy to do. He was really sophisticated, melodically." This explicitly mentions Neil Gust trying to absorb a particular musical element (melodic sensibility) from Elliott Smith. </accept>

